  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "A linear equation can have more than one solution. Explain why any number $x$ is a solution of the equation $2x+2(3-x)=6$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The left-hand side simplifies to $6$, so the equation is true for every real value of $x$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2x+2(3-x)&=2x+6-2x\\\\2x+2(3-x)&=6\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side equals the right-hand side for every real value of $x$. Therefore, every real number is a solution.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 36
  },
